<DocsLayout
  title="did:pki — PKI Bridge"
  description="Bridge traditional X.509 PKI certificates to decentralized identifiers. did:pki derives DIDs from certificate fingerprints."
  currentPath="/docs/identity/did-pki/"
>
  <h1>did:pki — PKI Bridge</h1>
  <p>
    <code>did:pki</code> bridges <strong>traditional PKI infrastructure</strong>
    (X.509 certificates) into the DID ecosystem. Organizations and governments
    that already have PKI can participate in decentralized identity without
    replacing their existing certificate infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h2>Format</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="DID">
    <Fragment set:html={`did:pki:&lt;fingerprint&gt;`} />
  </CodeBlock>
  <p>
    The <code>&lt;fingerprint&gt;</code> is derived from the X.509 certificate's
    SHA-256 fingerprint, hex-encoded and lowercased.
  </p>

  <h2>How It Works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Take an existing X.509 certificate (e.g., a Firma Digital certificate from Costa Rica's SINPE-FD)</li>
    <li>Compute the SHA-256 fingerprint of the DER-encoded certificate</li>
    <li>The DID becomes <code>did:pki:&lt;hex-fingerprint&gt;</code></li>
    <li>Resolution discovers the certificate and extracts the public key into a DID Document</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>DID Document Structure</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="JSON">
    <Fragment set:html={`<span class="c-br">{</span>
  <span class="c-attr">"@context"</span>: <span class="c-br">[</span><span class="c-str">"https://www.w3.org/ns/did/v1"</span><span class="c-br">]</span>,
  <span class="c-attr">"id"</span>: <span class="c-str">"did:pki:a1b2c3d4e5f6..."</span>,
  <span class="c-attr">"verificationMethod"</span>: <span class="c-br">[</span><span class="c-br">{</span>
    <span class="c-attr">"id"</span>: <span class="c-str">"did:pki:a1b2c3d4e5f6...#key-1"</span>,
    <span class="c-attr">"type"</span>: <span class="c-str">"JsonWebKey2020"</span>,
    <span class="c-attr">"controller"</span>: <span class="c-str">"did:pki:a1b2c3d4e5f6..."</span>,
    <span class="c-attr">"publicKeyJwk"</span>: <span class="c-br">{</span>
      <span class="c-attr">"kty"</span>: <span class="c-str">"EC"</span>,
      <span class="c-attr">"crv"</span>: <span class="c-str">"P-256"</span>,
      <span class="c-attr">"x"</span>: <span class="c-str">"..."</span>,
      <span class="c-attr">"y"</span>: <span class="c-str">"..."</span>
    <span class="c-br">}</span>
  <span class="c-br">}</span><span class="c-br">]</span>,
  <span class="c-attr">"authentication"</span>: <span class="c-br">[</span><span class="c-str">"did:pki:a1b2c3d4e5f6...#key-1"</span><span class="c-br">]</span>,
  <span class="c-attr">"assertionMethod"</span>: <span class="c-br">[</span><span class="c-str">"did:pki:a1b2c3d4e5f6...#key-1"</span><span class="c-br">]</span>
<span class="c-br">}</span>`} />
  </CodeBlock>

  <h2>Use Cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Government PKI</strong> — Costa Rica's Firma Digital, SINPE-FD certificates, and other national PKI systems can participate as DID-based issuers and verifiers</li>
    <li><strong>Enterprise certificates</strong> — organizations with existing TLS or code-signing certificates can derive DIDs without new key generation</li>
    <li><strong>Certificate transparency</strong> — bridge CT logs into the DID ecosystem for auditable identity</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="info">
    <p>
      <code>did:pki</code> does not replace or compete with PKI. It
      <strong>bridges</strong> existing PKI trust into the DID world, letting
      the same certificate that signs a government document also anchor a
      Verifiable Credential.
    </p>
  </Callout>

